\section{Which TMRCA you get}{

\code{tmrca} is a \strong{depth}: ticks from the older of the two sampled genomes back to their
common ancestor, so an identical relationship returns an identical number wherever in the
run it occurred -- a clonal parent-child pair gives 0 whether it happened on tick 10 or
tick 390. \code{tmrca_node_time} is the raw tskit node time of that ancestor, i.e. ticks before
the \strong{end of the run}; that is what \code{tree.tmrca()} returns natively and it is dominated by
when the pair was sampled rather than by how related it is. Use \code{tmrca} for relatedness.
}

\section{Python}{

The IBD and ARG functions read a run's tree sequence with Python's tskit, through the
reticulate package. Either of two routes works. \emph{Automatic}: with no Python chosen, reticulate
sets up its own environment with tskit (>= 1.0) and numpy the first time one of these
functions runs; this needs internet once. \emph{Your own Python}: set \code{RETICULATE_PYTHON} to a
Python that has those modules (in \verb{~/.Renviron} to keep it) before the first such call of a
session. A function that cannot reach them stops with an error of class
\code{malariafwd_no_python} that says what to do, and \code{\link[=check_setup]{check_setup()}} reports what is found.
